/*! \page developer_backend_device_page Device Arrays

\ref developer_backend_build_page  (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_complex_page   (Next)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

In code that uses a GPU, arrays may be allocated either in the main
memory of the CPU host or in the global memory of the GPU device. 
Arrays that are allocated in GPU memory when using a CUDA backend are 
declared in PSCF as instances of specializations of a class template 
named Pscf::DeviceArray. The class template Pscf::DeviceArray takes 
two template paramters, and is declared as:
\code
   template <typename Data, class T> class DeviceArray
\endcode
The argument of template parameter Data is the type of each element 
in the array. The argument of parameter T must be a backend identifer 
class. Partial specializations of the DeviceArray template with 
T=Pscf::CUT and T=Pscf::CPT are defined to be used with a CUDA GPU 
backend or a C++ CPU backend, respectively.

Definining DeviceArray as a class template that takes a backend identifier 
class as a template parameter allows a DeviceArray object to be used in 
backend-independent template code within the definitions of other class 
templates that also take T as a template parameter.  In such templates, 
an instance of DeviceArray<Data,T> can be used to represent an array with 
elements of type Data that is allocated in GPU memory in template 
specializations that use a CUDA backend (T=Pscf::CUT) but that is allocated 
in CPU main memory in specializations that use a C++ backend (T=Pscf::CPT). 

Partial specializations of DeviceArray for different values of T are 
defined in different files, as discussed below.

<b> CUDA backend </b>:

The partial specialization of DeviceArray with T=Pscf::CUT is a wrapper
for an underlying array that is allocated in global GPU device memory,
and is defined in the file src/pscf/backend/cuda/DeviceArray.h.  For
example, the specialization Pscf::DeviceArray<double,Pscf::CUT> is thus 
an array of double precision numbers that is allocated in GPU memory.  

Specializations of DeviceArray<Data,T> with T=Pscf::CUT do not allow 
access to individual elements of the array from the CPU host, and 
instead only allow array elements to be accessed within CUDA kernels. 
Mathematical operations on such arrays must thus be performed by 
CUDA kernels. 

<b> C++ backend </b>:


The partial specialization of DeviceArray with T=Pscf::CPT is a wrapper 
for a conventional array that is allocated in CPU host memory, and is
is defined in the file src/pscf/backend/cpp/DeviceArray.h.  For example, 
an instance of class DeviceArray<double,Pscf::CPT> is thus an array of 
double precision numbers that is allocated in CPU memory.  One technical 
feature of a DeviceArray<Data,Pscf::CPT>, which distinguishes it from a 
DArray<Data> container, is that a DeviceArray<Data,Pscf::CPT> allocates 
memory using functions provided by the FFTW fast Fourier transform 
library, which aligns memory in a manner that is designed to optimize
the performance of that library. 

Each specialization of DeviceArray<Data,T> with T=CPT is indirectly 
derived from a specialization of Util::Array<Data> with the same argument
for the typename parameter Data. The Util::Array template provides an 
interface for access to individual elements on the CPU host via a 
subscript operator.  

<BR>
